// worked solutions. each newline becomes a <br>.
window.QUAL_SOLUTIONS_DATA = {
 "groups": {
  "1": "$80 = 2^4 \\cdot 5$.\n\nWe recall Sylow's theorem. Let $|G| = p^a m$ with $p \\nmid m$. Then a Sylow $p$-subgroup has order $p^a$, all Sylow $p$-subgroups are conjugate in $G$, and their number $n_p$ satisfies $n_p \\equiv 1 \\pmod p$ and $n_p \\mid m$. A Sylow $p$-subgroup is normal in $G$ exactly when $n_p = 1$.\n\nTake $p = 5$, so $m = 16$. Then $n_5 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 5$ and $n_5 \\mid 16$ leave $n_5 \\in \\{1, 16\\}$.\n\n<b>Case 1:</b> If $n_5 = 1$, then the Sylow $5$-subgroup is normal in $G$ and we are done.\n\n<b>Case 2:</b> If $n_5 = 16$, we count the elements of order $5$. Each Sylow $5$-subgroup has prime order, so two distinct ones meet only in the identity, and each contains $4$ elements of order $5$. That gives $16 \\cdot 4 = 64$ elements of order $5$.\n\nOnly $80 - 64 = 16$ elements remain, the identity among them. A Sylow $2$-subgroup needs all $16$, so there is room for exactly one. Hence $n_2 = 1$ and the Sylow $2$-subgroup is normal in $G$.\n\nIn either case some Sylow subgroup is normal in $G$, so $G$ is not simple.",
  "2": "$132 = 2^2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 11$. Suppose $G$ is simple. Then no Sylow subgroup is normal in $G$, so the number $n_p$ of Sylow $p$-subgroups cannot equal $1$ for any prime $p$ dividing $|G|$.\n\nSylow's theorem gives $n_{11} \\mid 12$ and $n_{11} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{11}$, so $n_{11} \\in \\{1, 12\\}$. Simplicity rules out $1$, so $n_{11} = 12$. The Sylow $11$-subgroups have prime order and any two of them meet only in the identity, so they contribute $12 \\cdot 10 = 120$ elements of order $11$.\n\nSimilarly $n_3 \\mid 44$ and $n_3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$ give $n_3 \\in \\{1, 4, 22\\}$. We take the three cases in turn.\n\n<b>Case 1:</b> $n_3 = 22$ would add $22 \\cdot 2 = 44$ elements of order $3$, and $120 + 44 = 164 > 132$. This is impossible.\n\n<b>Case 2:</b> $n_3 = 1$ makes the Sylow $3$-subgroup normal in $G$, which is ruled out.\n\n<b>Case 3:</b> $n_3 = 4$ lets $G$ act by conjugation on its four Sylow $3$-subgroups, giving $G \\hookrightarrow S_4$. (The action is transitive, so the kernel is a proper normal subgroup of $G$, and simplicity makes it trivial.) But $|G| = 132 \\nmid 24 = |S_4|$, so this is impossible.\n\nEvery case fails, so no group of order $132$ is simple.",
  "3": "$72 = 2^3 \\cdot 3^2$. Assume $G$ is simple.\n\nSylow's theorem says the number $n_3$ of Sylow $3$-subgroups of $G$ satisfies $n_3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$ and $n_3 \\mid 8$, so $n_3 \\in \\{1, 4\\}$.\n\n$n_3 = 1$ makes the Sylow $3$-subgroup normal in $G$, which simplicity forbids. So $n_3 = 4$.\n\nNow $G$ acts by conjugation on its four Sylow $3$-subgroups. The action is transitive, so the map $\\rho : G \\to S_4$ is nontrivial and $\\ker\\rho \\ne G$. Then $G/\\ker\\rho \\hookrightarrow S_4$ forces $|G|/|\\ker\\rho| \\le 24$, hence $|\\ker\\rho| \\ge 72/24 = 3$.\n\nSo $\\ker\\rho$ is a proper nontrivial normal subgroup of $G$, contradicting simplicity. No group of order $72$ is simple.",
  "4": "$402 = 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 67$ with $67$ prime.\n\nWrite $|G| = 67 \\cdot 6$. Sylow's theorem says the number $n_{67}$ of Sylow $67$-subgroups of $G$ satisfies $n_{67} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{67}$ and $n_{67} \\mid 6$. The only value at most $6$ that is $\\equiv 1 \\pmod{67}$ is $n_{67} = 1$.\n\nSo the Sylow $67$-subgroup is normal in $G$, and $G$ is not simple.",
  "5": "$|G| = 3 \\cdot 2^n$ is an order of the form $p^a q^b$ for two primes $p$ and $q$.\n\nBurnside's theorem says that every finite group whose order has at most two distinct prime divisors is solvable. (D&F 19.2)\n\nThe only primes dividing $|G| = 2^n \\cdot 3$ are $2$ and $3$, so $G$ is solvable. That is the whole argument.",
  "6": "$U_n$ is the group of invertible upper triangular $n \\times n$ matrices over a field $F$. A group $G$ is solvable when its derived series $G \\supseteq G' \\supseteq G'' \\supseteq \\cdots$ reaches $1$.\n\nFirst, the map $U_n \\to (F^\\times)^n$ taking a matrix to its diagonal entries is a homomorphism onto an abelian group, because the diagonal of a product of upper triangular matrices is the product of the diagonals. Its kernel is the group of unitriangular matrices, that is, the upper triangular matrices with ones on the diagonal. So $[U_n, U_n]$ is contained in the unitriangular matrices.\n\nFilter the unitriangular matrices by distance above the diagonal. Let $U_n^{(k)} = I + (\\text{strictly upper, zeros on the first } k-1 \\text{ superdiagonals})$.\n\nEach quotient $U_n^{(k)} / U_n^{(k+1)}$ is abelian, since entries on a single superdiagonal add.\n\nA commutator moves up one superdiagonal. For strictly upper triangular, hence nilpotent, matrices $N_1, N_2$ we have $[I + N_1, I + N_2] = I + [N_1, N_2] + (\\text{higher order})$, and the bracket lands one diagonal higher. So $[U_n^{(k)}, U_n^{(k)}] \\subseteq U_n^{(k+1)}$. Also $U_n^{(1)}$ is the unitriangular group, so the first step gives $[U_n, U_n] \\subseteq U_n^{(1)}$.\n\nThe derived series therefore moves up the superdiagonals, and after at most $n$ steps it lies in $U_n^{(n)} = \\{I\\}$. So $U_n$ is solvable.",
  "7": "The commutator subgroup $[G, G]$ is generated by the commutators $[x, y] = xyx^{-1}y^{-1}$, and $G$ is perfect when $G = [G, G]$.\n\nOne inclusion is immediate. Since $\\det[x, y] = 1$, we have $[GL_2, GL_2] \\subseteq SL_2(\\mathbb{C})$.\n\nFor the reverse inclusion, set $d = \\mathrm{diag}(\\lambda, \\lambda^{-1})$ and $u_b = \\begin{pmatrix}1 & b \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{pmatrix}$, and compute\n$$[d, u_b] = \\begin{pmatrix}1 & (\\lambda^2 - 1)b \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{pmatrix}.$$\nTaking $\\lambda = 2$ and $b = a/3$ gives any upper transvection $\\begin{pmatrix}1 & a \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{pmatrix}$, and the lower transvections are obtained in the same way. These transvections generate $SL_2(\\mathbb{C})$ by row reduction over the field.\n\nSo $[GL_2, GL_2] = SL_2(\\mathbb{C})$. The same commutators already lie in $[SL_2, SL_2]$, so $SL_2 = [SL_2, SL_2]$ is perfect.\n\nThen $SL_2^{(k)} = SL_2$ for every $k$, and the derived series never reaches $1$. So the group is not solvable.",
  "8": "Here $|G| = p^3$ with $p$ an odd prime, and we write $Z$ for the center $Z(G)$. The claim $Z(G) = [G, G]$ is meant for nonabelian $G$. (If $G$ is abelian then $Z = G$ while $[G,G] = 1$, so assume $G$ is nonabelian.)\n\nThe class equation $|G| = |Z| + \\sum [G : C_G(x_i)]$ has one term for each noncentral conjugacy class, with representative $x_i$, and each index is a power of $p$ bigger than $1$. So $p \\mid |Z|$, which forces $Z \\ne 1$ and $|Z| \\in \\{p, p^2, p^3\\}$.\n\n<b>Case 1:</b> $|Z| = p^3$ would make $G = Z$ abelian, which is excluded.\n\n<b>Case 2:</b> $|Z| = p^2$ makes $G/Z$ cyclic of order $p$. Take $gZ$ generating $G/Z$. Then every element of $G$ is $g^i z$ with $z \\in Z$, and any two such elements commute, so $G$ is abelian, which is excluded.\n\nSo $|Z| = p$.\n\nSince $G$ is nonabelian, $[G, G] \\ne 1$. The quotient $G/Z$ has order $p^2$, hence is abelian, so every commutator is trivial in it, that is $[G, G] \\subseteq Z$. Now $1 \\ne [G, G] \\subseteq Z$ with $|Z| = p$ forces $[G, G] = Z$.\n\nThat is $Z(G) = [G, G]$.",
  "9": "Let $H, K \\trianglelefteq G$ with $G/H$ and $G/K$ nilpotent. A group $G$ is nilpotent when its lower central series $G \\supseteq [G, G] \\supseteq [[G, G], G] \\supseteq \\cdots$ reaches $1$.\n\nWe use three standard facts. A subgroup of a nilpotent group is nilpotent. A finite product of nilpotent groups is nilpotent, since the lower central series of a product is the product of the series. And the diagonal map\n$$G/(H \\cap K) \\to G/H \\times G/K, \\qquad g(H \\cap K) \\mapsto (gH, gK)$$\nis a well-defined homomorphism with kernel $(H \\cap K)/(H \\cap K) = 1$, hence injective.\n\nSo $G/(H \\cap K)$ embeds in $G/H \\times G/K$. That product is nilpotent, being a product of two nilpotent groups, and a subgroup of a nilpotent group is nilpotent. Therefore $G/(H \\cap K)$ is nilpotent.",
  "10": "If $\\mathrm{Aut}(G) = 1$ then every inner automorphism $x \\mapsto gxg^{-1}$ is trivial, so $G$ is abelian. (Concretely, $\\mathrm{Inn}(G) \\cong G/Z(G)$ is a subgroup of $\\mathrm{Aut}(G)$, which forces $G = Z(G)$.)\n\nSince $G$ is abelian, inversion $x \\mapsto x^{-1}$ is an automorphism of $G$. It has to be the identity, so $x = x^{-1}$, that is $x^2 = 1$, for every $x$ in $G$. This is the conclusion asked for, that $G$ is abelian with every element of order at most $2$.\n\nGoing further, $G$ is then a vector space over $\\mathbb{F}_2$, say of dimension $n$, and $\\mathrm{Aut}(G) = GL(n, \\mathbb{F}_2)$. That group is trivial only for $n = 0$ or $n = 1$, since $|GL(n, \\mathbb{F}_2)| > 1$ once $n \\ge 2$. (If $G$ is infinite, Zorn's lemma gives a basis of $G$ over $\\mathbb{F}_2$, and swapping two basis vectors is a nontrivial automorphism.) So in fact $G \\cong 1$ or $\\mathbb{Z}/2$, and both of these have trivial automorphism group.",
  "11": "$|S_{28}| = 28!$. The order of a Sylow $7$-subgroup is the full power of $7$ dividing $28!$.\n\nWe count that power directly. The multiples of $7$ in $\\{1, \\dots, 28\\}$ are $7, 14, 21, 28$. There are four of them, and none is a multiple of $49$. Each contributes one factor of $7$, so $7^4 \\mid 28!$ exactly, and a Sylow $7$-subgroup has order $7^4$.\n\nWe construct one. Split $\\{1, \\dots, 28\\}$ into four blocks of $7$ and let $\\sigma_1, \\dots, \\sigma_4$ be $7$-cycles, one on each block. They have disjoint supports, so they commute and each has order $7$. So $P = \\langle \\sigma_1, \\dots, \\sigma_4 \\rangle$ is the product of four commuting cyclic groups of order $7$, and it has order $7^4$.\n\nThat matches the Sylow order, so $P$ is a Sylow $7$-subgroup, and every Sylow $7$-subgroup is conjugate to it in $S_{28}$. So the Sylow $7$-subgroup is $(\\mathbb{Z}/7)^4$, which is abelian of exponent $7$.",
  "12": "$F_2 = \\langle a, b \\rangle$. Map it onto $\\mathbb{Z}$ by $\\varphi : a \\mapsto 0$ and $b \\mapsto 1$, and let $K = \\ker\\varphi$, the set of words whose total $b$-exponent is zero. Then $H = \\langle bab^{-1}, b^2ab^{-2}, b^3ab^{-3} \\rangle \\le K$.\n\nThe Nielsen-Schreier theorem says every subgroup of a free group is free. The index $[F_2 : K]$ is infinite, so instead of a rank count we use Schreier generators directly. Take the right transversal $T = \\{b^n : n \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\}$ and write $\\overline{g}$ for the element of $T$ in the coset $Kg$. The Schreier generators are the nontrivial elements $t x \\overline{tx}^{-1}$ with $t \\in T$, $x \\in \\{a, b\\}$. For $x = b$ these are $b^n b \\, b^{-(n+1)} = 1$, and for $x = a$ they are $b^n a b^{-n}$, so $K$ is free on $\\{b^n a b^{-n} : n \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\}$.\n\nThe three given elements $bab^{-1}, b^2ab^{-2}, b^3ab^{-3}$ are three distinct members of that free basis. Any subset of a free basis freely generates a free group, so $H \\cong F_3$."
 },
 "rings": {
  "1": "Write $\\mathrm{char}(R)$ for the smallest $n > 0$ with $n \\cdot 1 = 0$, or $0$ if there is none.\n\n<b>Part (a):</b> Put $x = 2 \\cdot 1$ into $x^3 = x$. Then $(2 \\cdot 1)^3 = 8 \\cdot 1$ equals $2 \\cdot 1$, so $6 \\cdot 1 = 0$ and $\\mathrm{char}(R) \\mid 6$.\n\n<b>Part (c):</b> Suppose $\\mathrm{char}(R) = 2$. Using $x^3 = x$,\n$$(x + 1)^3 = x^3 + 3x^2 + 3x + 1 = x + x^2 + x + 1 = x^2 + 1,$$\nwhile also $(x + 1)^3 = x + 1$. Comparing gives $x^2 + 1 = x + 1$, so $x^2 = x$. So the ring is Boolean, and a Boolean ring is commutative. (Expanding $(x + y)^2 = x + y$ gives $xy = -yx$, and $(x + x)^2 = x + x$ gives $2x = 0$, so $-yx = yx$.)\n\n<b>Part (b):</b> Both characteristics occur. The field $\\mathbb{F}_2$ has characteristic $2$ and satisfies $x^3 = x$. The field $\\mathbb{F}_3$ has characteristic $3$, and Fermat's little theorem gives $x^3 = x$ for every $x$ in $\\mathbb{F}_3$.",
  "2": "A Boolean ring is a ring with $x^2 = x$ for all $x$. We claim that it is commutative of characteristic $2$.\n\nFirst we find the characteristic. Expand $(x + x)^2 = x + x$. Since $(2x)^2 = 4x^2 = 4x$ and also $(2x)^2 = 2x$, we get $4x = 2x$, so $2x = 0$. Every element is its own negative, and the characteristic divides $2$.\n\nNext we prove commutativity. Expand $(x + y)^2 = x + y$ as $x^2 + xy + yx + y^2 = x + xy + yx + y$, which leaves $xy + yx = 0$, that is $xy = -yx$. By the first step $-yx = yx$, so $xy = yx$. So the ring is commutative.",
  "3": "$I = (x^2, xy) \\subset \\mathbb{Q}[x, y]$.\n\nThe ideal $I$ is not prime. A prime ideal $\\mathfrak p$ satisfies $ab \\in \\mathfrak p \\Rightarrow a \\in \\mathfrak p$ or $b \\in \\mathfrak p$. Here $x \\cdot y = xy \\in I$, yet $x \\notin I$ and $y \\notin I$, since every monomial of every element of $I$ is divisible by $x^2$ or $xy$, so no element of $I$ has a term of degree $1$.\n\nNext we find the primes containing $I$. Such a prime $\\mathfrak p$ contains $x^2$, and primeness gives $x \\in \\mathfrak p$, so $\\mathfrak p \\supseteq (x)$. The correspondence theorem matches the primes containing $(x)$ with the primes of $\\mathbb{Q}[x, y]/(x) \\cong \\mathbb{Q}[y]$. So the primes containing $I$ are $(x)$ and the ideals $(x, g(y))$ with $g$ irreducible in $\\mathbb{Q}[y]$.\n\nTwo natural choices are the following. The ideal $(x)$ is prime, since $\\mathbb{Q}[x, y]/(x) \\cong \\mathbb{Q}[y]$ is a domain, but it is not maximal, since that quotient is not a field. The ideal $(x, y)$ is maximal, since $\\mathbb{Q}[x, y]/(x, y) \\cong \\mathbb{Q}$ is a field.",
  "4": "Setup: Let $f \\in R$ be a nonzerodivisor on $R$ and let $S = \\{1, f, f^2, \\dots\\}$ be the multiplicative set it generates. The localization $S^{-1}R$ consists of fractions $r/s$ with $s \\in S$, where $r/s = r'/s'$ iff $t(rs' - r's) = 0$ for some $t \\in S$. Since $f$ is a nonzerodivisor on $R$, this is exactly $rs' = r's$.\n\nUniversal property: The map $R \\to S^{-1}R$ inverts every element of $S$, and every ring map $R \\to T$ sending $S$ into the units of $T$ factors uniquely through it.\n\nWe claim $S^{-1}R \\cong R[x]/(fx - 1)$. Define $\\varphi : R[x] \\to S^{-1}R$ by $r \\mapsto r/1$ and $x \\mapsto 1/f$. Then $\\varphi(fx - 1) = f/f - 1 = 0$, so $\\varphi$ annihilates $(fx - 1)$ and descends to a map $\\bar\\varphi$ on the quotient, with $\\bar\\varphi(r x^n) = r/f^n$. This map is onto.\n\nWe build the inverse with the universal property. Write $\\bar x$ for the image of $x$ in $R[x]/(fx - 1)$. The map $R \\to R[x]/(fx - 1)$ sends $f$ to a unit, since $f \\bar x = 1$, so it factors through $S^{-1}R$ as $\\psi(r/f^n) = r \\bar x^n$. Then $\\bar\\varphi \\psi$ and $\\psi \\bar\\varphi$ are the identity on the generators ($r/1$ and $1/f$ on one side, $r$ and $\\bar x$ on the other), so the rings are isomorphic. This part never uses that $f$ is a nonzerodivisor on $R$, so the isomorphism holds for any $f$. (D&F 15.4)",
  "5": "Goal: Let $R$ be commutative. Then the set of nilpotents $\\{z : z^k = 0 \\text{ for some } k\\}$ is closed under addition. (Closure under multiplication by elements of $R$ is clear, since $x^m = 0$ gives $(rx)^m = r^m x^m = 0$.)\n\nLemma. If $x^m = 0$ and $y^n = 0$, then $x + y$ is nilpotent. The binomial theorem is valid because $R$ is commutative, and it gives\n$$(x + y)^{m+n} = \\sum_{k=0}^{m+n} \\binom{m+n}{k} x^k y^{m+n-k}.$$\nIn each term either $k \\ge m$ or $m + n - k \\ge n$, since both failing would need $k < m$ and $k > m$ simultaneously. So every term has a vanishing factor, and $(x + y)^{m+n} = 0$.\n\nHence the nilpotents are closed under addition and under multiplication by $R$, so they form an ideal, and in particular $x + y$ is nilpotent.",
  "6": "Theorem. Let $R$ be a commutative ring with $1$. Then $\\bigcap_{\\mathfrak p \\text{ prime}} \\mathfrak p$ equals the set of nilpotents of $R$.\n\n$(\\supseteq)$ If $x^n = 0$, then $x^n \\in \\mathfrak p$ for every prime $\\mathfrak p$, and primeness applied to $x \\cdot x^{n-1}$, followed by induction, gives $x \\in \\mathfrak p$. So every nilpotent lies in every prime.\n\n$(\\subseteq)$ We prove the contrapositive. If $x$ is not nilpotent, then $S = \\{1, x, x^2, \\dots\\}$ does not contain $0$. Let $\\Sigma$ be the set of ideals disjoint from $S$, ordered by inclusion. It is nonempty (it contains $0$), and the union of a chain is again an ideal disjoint from $S$, so every chain has an upper bound. Zorn's lemma gives a maximal element $\\mathfrak p$ of $\\Sigma$, and $\\mathfrak p$ is proper since $1 \\in S$.\n\nThe ideal $\\mathfrak p$ is prime. If $a, b \\notin \\mathfrak p$, then $\\mathfrak p + (a)$ and $\\mathfrak p + (b)$ strictly contain $\\mathfrak p$, so each meets $S$, say $x^m \\in \\mathfrak p + (a)$ and $x^n \\in \\mathfrak p + (b)$. Multiplying, $x^{m+n} \\in \\mathfrak p + (ab)$, so $\\mathfrak p + (ab)$ meets $S$ and $ab \\notin \\mathfrak p$.\n\nSo $\\mathfrak p$ is a prime not containing $x$, and $x$ is not in $\\bigcap \\mathfrak p$. (D&F 15.2)",
  "7": "First we find the units of $K[[x]]$ for $K$ a commutative ring with $1$. The series $f = \\sum a_n x^n$ is a unit of $K[[x]]$ iff $a_0$ is a unit of $K$.\n\n$(\\Rightarrow)$ If $fg = 1$ with $g = \\sum b_n x^n$, the constant terms give $a_0 b_0 = 1$.\n\n$(\\Leftarrow)$ Solve $fg = 1$ one coefficient at a time. Set $b_0 = a_0^{-1}$, then $b_n = -a_0^{-1} \\sum_{k=1}^{n} a_k b_{n-k}$, which builds an inverse.\n\nNow let $K$ be a field. The nonunits are exactly $\\{f : a_0 = 0\\} = (x)$, which is an ideal, so $K[[x]]$ is local with maximal ideal $(x)$.\n\nTo see that every ideal is principal, write any nonzero $f = x^n u$ where $n$ is the least index with $a_n \\ne 0$, so $u$ has a unit constant term and is a unit. Given a nonzero ideal $I$, let $n_0$ be the least $n$ such that $I$ contains some $x^n u$ with $u$ a unit. Dividing by the unit gives $x^{n_0} \\in I$, and any $f = x^n u \\in I$ has $n \\ge n_0$, hence $f \\in (x^{n_0})$. So $I = (x^{n_0})$, and $K[[x]]$ is a PID, in fact a DVR. (D&F 7.2)",
  "8": "The ideals of $\\mathbb{Z}/n$ correspond to the divisors $d \\mid n$ via $d \\mapsto (d)/(n)$, and such an ideal is maximal iff $d$ is prime. For $\\mathbb{Z}/100$ with $100 = 2^2 5^2$ the maximal ideals are $(2)$ and $(5)$, with quotient fields $\\mathbb{Z}/2$ and $\\mathbb{Z}/5$.\n\nAs a check, the Chinese remainder theorem gives $\\mathbb{Z}/100 \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/4 \\times \\mathbb{Z}/25$, a product of two local rings.\n\nNow localize at $S = \\{100^n\\}$. A prime $(p)$ of $\\mathbb{Z}$ survives in $S^{-1}\\mathbb{Z}$ iff $(p) \\cap S = \\varnothing$, that is iff $p \\nmid 100$, that is iff $p \\ne 2, 5$. Since $\\mathbb{Z}$ is a PID, those surviving $(p)$ are exactly the maximal ideals of $S^{-1}\\mathbb{Z}$. (D&F 7.5, 15.4)",
  "9": "$R = C[a, b]$ is the ring of continuous real functions on the interval, and $M$ is a maximal ideal of $R$. We claim $M = M_c := \\{f : f(c) = 0\\}$ for some $c$.\n\nEach $M_c$ is the kernel of the evaluation map $\\mathrm{ev}_c : R \\to \\mathbb{R}$, a surjection onto a field, so $R/M_c \\cong \\mathbb{R}$ and $M_c$ is maximal. This proves the converse direction.\n\nFor the forward direction, suppose $M$ is contained in no $M_c$. Then for each $c$ there is $f_c \\in M$ with $f_c(c) \\ne 0$, and by continuity of $f_c$ at $c$ we have $f_c^2 > 0$ on an open neighborhood $U_c$ of $c$. The sets $U_c$ cover $[a, b]$, and $[a, b]$ is compact, so there is a finite subcover $U_{c_1}, \\dots, U_{c_k}$. Then $g = \\sum_j f_{c_j}^2 \\in M$ is strictly positive everywhere, so $1/g \\in R$ and $g$ is a unit, which forces $M = R$. That contradicts $M$ being proper.\n\nSo $M \\subseteq M_c$ for some $c$, and maximality of $M$ gives $M = M_c$.",
  "10": "The plan is as follows. Euclidean $\\Rightarrow$ PID $\\Rightarrow$ UFD, so to rule out Euclidean it is enough to rule out UFD. (In a Euclidean domain every nonzero ideal is generated by an element of least norm, and a PID is a UFD because it is Noetherian and its irreducibles are prime.)\n\nIn $\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{-13}]$ the norm $N(a + b\\sqrt{-13}) = a^2 + 13b^2$ is multiplicative. Units have norm $1$, and $a^2 + 13b^2 = 1$ forces $(a, b) = (\\pm 1, 0)$, so the units are $\\{\\pm 1\\}$. If $N(\\alpha)$ is a rational prime then $\\alpha$ is irreducible in $\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{-13}]$, since a proper factorization of $\\alpha$ would factor the norm nontrivially.\n\nThere are two factorizations of $14$,\n$$14 = 2 \\cdot 7 = (1 + \\sqrt{-13})(1 - \\sqrt{-13}).$$\nNo element has norm $2$ or $7$. Indeed, if $|b| \\ge 1$ then the norm satisfies $a^2 + 13b^2 \\ge 13$, so any element of norm $2$ or $7$ has $b = 0$, leaving $a^2 \\in \\{2, 7\\}$, which has no integer solution. So $2$ and $7$ (norms $4, 49$) and $1 \\pm \\sqrt{-13}$ (norm $14$) are all irreducible in $\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{-13}]$. The two sides share no associates, since associates have the same norm, and the norms $4$ and $49$ differ from $14$.\n\nSo $\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{-13}]$ is not a UFD, hence not a PID, hence not Euclidean.",
  "11": "In $\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{10}]$ the norm $N(a + b\\sqrt{10}) = a^2 - 10b^2$ is multiplicative. Units have norm $\\pm 1$, since an inverse forces $N(\\alpha) N(\\alpha^{-1}) = 1$ in $\\mathbb{Z}$. If $|N(\\alpha)|$ is prime then $\\alpha$ is irreducible in $\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{10}]$.\n\nThere are two factorizations of $6$,\n$$6 = 2 \\cdot 3 = (4 + \\sqrt{10})(4 - \\sqrt{10}), \\qquad N(4 \\pm \\sqrt{10}) = 16 - 10 = 6.$$\nNo element has norm $\\pm 2$ or $\\pm 3$. To see this, reduce mod $5$. There $a^2 - 10b^2 \\equiv a^2$, and the squares mod $5$ are $0, 1, 4$. But $\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 3$ are $\\equiv 2$ or $3 \\pmod 5$, so $a^2 - 10b^2$ can never equal any of them. So $2$ and $3$ (norms $4, 9$) and $4 \\pm \\sqrt{10}$ (norm $6$) are all irreducible in $\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{10}]$.\n\nThe two sides have norm multisets $\\{4, 9\\}$ and $\\{6, 6\\}$, so nothing on one side is an associate of anything on the other. So $\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{10}]$ is not a UFD.",
  "12": "$R = \\mathbb{C}[x^3, x^4] \\subset \\mathbb{C}[x]$. Its monomials are the $x^{3a + 4b}$, so $R$ contains monomials of every degree except $1, 2, 5$, the gaps of the numerical semigroup $\\langle 3, 4 \\rangle$. The units of $R$ are $\\mathbb{C}^\\times$, the nonzero constants.\n\nThere are two factorizations,\n$$x^{12} = (x^3)^4 = (x^4)^3.$$\nBoth $x^3$ and $x^4$ are irreducible in $R$, since a proper factor would have degree at least $3$ (there is nothing in degree $1$ or $2$), so a product of two proper factors has degree at least $6 > 4$. Also $x^3, x^4$ are not associates in $R$, since $x^4/x^3 = x \\notin R$.\n\nSo $x^{12}$ has two different irreducible factorizations, and $R$ is not a UFD.",
  "13": "The ring of integers of $\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt d)$ is $\\mathbb{Z}[\\tfrac{1 + \\sqrt d}{2}]$ when the squarefree $d \\equiv 1 \\pmod 4$, and $\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt d]$ otherwise. Here $d = -11 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 4$, so the ring of integers of $K = \\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{-11})$ is $\\mathcal{O}_K = \\mathbb{Z}[\\tfrac{1 + \\sqrt{-11}}{2}]$. It contains $\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{-11}]$.\n\nSet $\\alpha = \\tfrac{1 + \\sqrt{-11}}{2}$. It satisfies $\\alpha^2 - \\alpha + 3 = 0$, a monic equation over $\\mathbb{Z}$, so $\\alpha$ is integral over $\\mathbb{Z}$. But $\\alpha \\notin \\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{-11}]$, since its $\\sqrt{-11}$-coefficient is $1/2$. So $\\mathcal{O}_K$ is strictly larger than $\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{-11}]$.\n\nEvery UFD is integrally closed in its fraction field. Now $\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{-11}]$ and $\\mathcal{O}_K$ have the same fraction field $\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{-11})$, and $\\alpha$ is integral over $\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{-11}]$ without belonging to it. So $\\mathbb{Z}[\\sqrt{-11}]$ is not integrally closed in its fraction field, hence not a UFD."
 },
 "modules": {
  "1": "We want the abelian groups of order at most $100$ that are injective $\\mathbb{Z}/56$-modules.\n\n$56 = 8 \\cdot 7$ with $\\gcd(8, 7) = 1$, so $\\mathbb{Z}/56 \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/8 \\times \\mathbb{Z}/7$. Each factor is a local ring whose ideals form a single chain, and over such a ring injective, projective, and free all describe the same finitely generated modules. So over $\\mathbb{Z}/56$ the finitely generated injectives are the direct summands of free modules, namely $(\\mathbb{Z}/8)^a \\oplus (\\mathbb{Z}/7)^b$.\n\nCount the ones with $8^a 7^b \\le 100$.\n\nThe orders are $1, \\ 8, \\ 7, \\ 64, \\ 49, \\ 56$, for $(a, b) = (0,0), (1,0), (0,1), (2,0), (0,2), (1,1)$.\n\nThere are six of them. Over $\\mathbb{Z}/n$ injective and projective modules are the same, so these are also the six projective $\\mathbb{Z}/56$-modules of order at most $100$.",
  "2": "We want the abelian groups of order at most $125$ that are projective $\\mathbb{Z}/100$-modules.\n\n$100 = 4 \\cdot 25$ with $\\gcd(4, 25) = 1$, so $\\mathbb{Z}/100 \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/4 \\times \\mathbb{Z}/25$. Over a product of local rings a projective module is a summand of a free module, and each indecomposable summand is a copy of $\\mathbb{Z}/4$ or $\\mathbb{Z}/25$. So the projectives are $(\\mathbb{Z}/4)^a \\oplus (\\mathbb{Z}/25)^b$.\n\nCount those with $4^a 25^b \\le 125$.\n\n$a = 0$ gives orders $1, 25$.\n$a = 1$ gives orders $4, 100$.\n$a = 2$ gives order $16$.\n$a = 3$ gives order $64$.\n\nThere are six.",
  "3": "We want the abelian groups of order at most $200$ that are injective $\\mathbb{Z}/120$-modules.\n\n$120 = 8 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5$ with pairwise coprime factors, so $\\mathbb{Z}/120 \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/8 \\times \\mathbb{Z}/3 \\times \\mathbb{Z}/5$. As in the earlier counts each factor has its ideals in a single chain, so a module is injective exactly when it is a direct summand of a free module, and the injectives are $(\\mathbb{Z}/8)^a \\oplus (\\mathbb{Z}/3)^b \\oplus (\\mathbb{Z}/5)^c$. (A plain $\\mathbb{Z}/2$ is not injective here. Only the full local factor $\\mathbb{Z}/8$ is.)\n\nCount those with $8^a 3^b 5^c \\le 200$.\n\n$a = 0$ leaves $3^b 5^c \\le 200$, which has twelve solutions.\n$a = 1$ leaves $3^b 5^c \\le 25$, which has six solutions.\n$a = 2$ leaves $3^b 5^c \\le 3$, which has two solutions.\n$a \\ge 3$ has $8^3 = 512 > 200$, so there are none.\n\nThere are twenty in all, and these are also exactly the projective ones.",
  "4": "We want, up to isomorphism, the projective quotients of $R^2$ with $R = \\mathbb{Z}/12$.\n\n$12 = 4 \\cdot 3$ with $\\gcd(4, 3) = 1$, so $R \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/4 \\times \\mathbb{Z}/3$ and $R^2 \\cong (\\mathbb{Z}/4)^2 \\times (\\mathbb{Z}/3)^2$.\n\nA surjection $R^2 \\twoheadrightarrow P$ onto a projective module splits, so $P$ is a direct summand of $R^2$. Since $R = R_1 \\times R_2$ with each $R_i$ local, projectives factor as $P = P_1 \\times P_2$ with $P_i$ a summand of $R_i^2$. Over a local ring a finitely generated projective module is free, so $P_i$ is free of rank $0$, $1$, or $2$.\n\nConversely each such $P_1 \\times P_2$ is a summand, hence a quotient, of $R^2$. The two ranks are chosen independently, giving $3 \\times 3 = 9$ classes.",
  "5": "$M = R/(x^2, xy) \\oplus R/(xy, y^2)$ over $R = k[x^2, xy, y^2]$, the ring of polynomials in $k[x, y]$ whose monomials all have even degree. We want the set of ring elements that annihilate $M$.\n\n<b>Part (b):</b> A ring element $r$ annihilates $M$ iff it annihilates both summands, and $r$ annihilates $R/I$ iff $r \\in I$. So the annihilator is\n$$(x^2, xy) \\cap (xy, y^2),$$\nwith both ideals taken in $R$.\n\nWe compute the intersection with monomials. Both ideals are generated by monomials, so they are spanned by the monomials they contain, and a monomial of $R$ is $x^a y^b$ with $a + b$ even. It lies in $(x^2, xy)$ iff $a \\ge 1$ and $a + b \\ge 2$. If $a \\ge 2$ it is $x^2 \\cdot x^{a-2} y^b$, and if $a = 1$ then $b$ is odd and it is $xy \\cdot y^{b-1}$, with the cofactor in $R$ both times. By symmetry it lies in $(xy, y^2)$ iff $b \\ge 1$ and $a + b \\ge 2$.\n\nSo the intersection is spanned by the $x^a y^b$ with $a, b \\ge 1$, and each of these is $xy \\cdot x^{a-1} y^{b-1}$ with $x^{a-1} y^{b-1} \\in R$. So the annihilator is exactly $(xy)$, the ideal of $R$ generated by $xy$.\n\n<b>Part (a):</b> The annihilator is an ideal. It is closed under addition and under multiplication by $R$, since each of these operations preserves the property of annihilating $M$.",
  "6": "<b>Claim.</b> Let $V$ be a vector space over a field of characteristic $\\ne 2$. Then $V \\otimes V = \\mathrm{Sym}^2(V) \\oplus \\Lambda^2(V)$.\n\nThe swap $\\sigma(v \\otimes w) = w \\otimes v$ is a linear involution, that is, $\\sigma^2 = 1$. Its minimal polynomial divides $t^2 - 1 = (t - 1)(t + 1)$, which has distinct roots when the characteristic is not $2$, so $\\sigma$ is diagonalizable and $V \\otimes V$ splits into its $+1$ and $-1$ eigenspaces.\n\nThe $+1$ eigenspace is the symmetric part $\\mathrm{Sym}^2(V)$, and the $-1$ eigenspace is the alternating part $\\Lambda^2(V)$. (D&F write $\\mathcal{S}^2(V)$ and $\\bigwedge^2(V)$ and define them as quotients of $V \\otimes V$. When the characteristic is $\\ne 2$ the quotient maps restrict to isomorphisms from these eigenspaces, so the two descriptions agree.) Project onto the eigenspaces by averaging over the swap, $p_\\pm = \\tfrac12(1 \\pm \\sigma)$. These satisfy $p_\\pm^2 = p_\\pm$, $p_+ p_- = 0$, $p_+ + p_- = 1$, so $V \\otimes V = p_+(V \\otimes V) \\oplus p_-(V \\otimes V)$. (The factor $\\tfrac12$ is exactly where characteristic $\\ne 2$ is used.)\n\nEvery tensor splits accordingly,\n$$v \\otimes w = \\tfrac12(v \\otimes w + w \\otimes v) + \\tfrac12(v \\otimes w - w \\otimes v),$$\nwhere the first piece is symmetric and the second is alternating.",
  "7": "Main fact. For positive integers $m$ and $n$, $\\mathbb{Z}/m \\otimes_{\\mathbb{Z}} \\mathbb{Z}/n \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/\\gcd(m, n)$.\n\nProof. The tensor product is generated by $1 \\otimes 1$, since $a \\otimes b = ab(1 \\otimes 1)$. Bezout's identity lets us write $g = \\gcd(m, n) = \\alpha m + \\beta n$ with integers $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$. Then\n$$g(1 \\otimes 1) = \\alpha(m \\otimes 1) + \\beta(1 \\otimes n) = 0,$$\nso the group is cyclic of order dividing $g$. For the matching lower bound, the map $\\mathbb{Z}/m \\times \\mathbb{Z}/n \\to \\mathbb{Z}/g$, $(a, b) \\mapsto ab \\bmod g$, is well defined and bilinear, so it factors through the tensor product and sends $1 \\otimes 1 \\mapsto 1$. That map is onto, so the order is exactly $g$.\n\n<b>Part (a):</b> $\\gcd(3, 2) = 1$, so $\\mathbb{Z}/3 \\otimes \\mathbb{Z}/2 = 0$. Directly, $2(a \\otimes b) = a \\otimes 2b = 0$ and $3(a \\otimes b) = 0$, and coprimality forces $a \\otimes b = 0$.\n\n<b>Part (b):</b> $\\gcd(4, 2) = 2$, so $\\mathbb{Z}/4 \\otimes \\mathbb{Z}/2 \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/2$, via $a \\otimes b \\mapsto ab \\bmod 2$ with $1 \\otimes 1 \\mapsto 1$.",
  "8": "Take the multiplication-by-$x$ sequence of $\\mathbb{C}[x]$-modules,\n$$0 \\to \\mathbb{C}[x] \\xrightarrow{\\ \\cdot x\\ } \\mathbb{C}[x] \\to \\mathbb{C} \\to 0,$$\nwhere the right-hand map is evaluation at $0$. It is exact. Multiplication by $x$ is injective because $\\mathbb{C}[x]$ is a domain and $x \\ne 0$. Its image is $(x)$, and $\\mathbb{C}[x]/(x) \\cong \\mathbb{C}$ by evaluation, so the kernel equals the image at the middle term.\n\nIt does not split. A splitting would make $\\mathbb{C} = \\mathbb{C}[x]/(x)$ a submodule of the middle term $\\mathbb{C}[x]$, and $x$ acts as $0$ on it. So there would be a nonzero $f \\in \\mathbb{C}[x]$ with $xf = 0$. But $\\mathbb{C}[x]$ is a domain, so $xf = 0$ forces $f = 0$. So there is no splitting. (This is the general fact about $0 \\to I \\to R \\to R/I \\to 0$ over a domain, with $I = (x)$.)\n\nAnother example is $0 \\to (x)/(x^2) \\to \\mathbb{C}[x]/(x^2) \\to \\mathbb{C}[x]/(x) \\to 0$. Here both ends are $\\mathbb{C}$ and the middle term is $\\mathbb{C}^2$, so the dimensions match. But in the basis $1, x$ of the middle term, $x$ acts by $(c, d) \\mapsto (0, c)$, which is not $0$, while $x$ annihilates both ends. So the middle term cannot be their direct sum.",
  "9": "Let $R$ be a domain and $I$ a nonzero proper ideal, viewed as an $R$-module.\n\nFirst, $I$ is free iff it is principal. Any two nonzero elements $a, b \\in I$ satisfy the nontrivial relation $b \\cdot a - a \\cdot b = 0$, so a free basis has at most one element. If $I$ is free it is then free of rank $1$, that is $I = (g)$.\n\nNow consider the sequence $0 \\to I \\to R \\to R/I \\to 0$. A splitting would give $R = I \\oplus M$ for a submodule $M$ of $R$, and the projection onto $I$ along $M$ is an idempotent $e \\in \\mathrm{End}_R(R) = R$, so $e^2 = e$. In a domain $e^2 = e$ gives $e(e - 1) = 0$, so $e = 0$ or $e = 1$, which forces $I = 0$ or $I = R$. Both are excluded.\n\nSo the sequence never splits.",
  "10": "Read the three generators $(2,2,2,2), (2,4,8,0), (8,8,0,4)$ of $H \\le \\mathbb{Z}^4$ as the columns of a $4 \\times 3$ matrix\n$$M = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 \\end{pmatrix},$$\nso $M : \\mathbb{Z}^3 \\to \\mathbb{Z}^4$ has image $H$ and the quotient is its cokernel. That is the exact sequence\n$$0 \\to \\mathbb{Z}^3 \\xrightarrow{\\ M\\ } \\mathbb{Z}^4 \\to \\mathbb{Z}^4/H \\to 0.$$\nThe generators are the columns, not the rows.\n\nWe use the Smith normal form over $\\mathbb{Z}$. Integer row and column operations (each of determinant $\\pm 1$) reduce $M$ to a diagonal matrix $\\mathrm{diag}(d_1, d_2, \\dots)$ with $d_1 \\mid d_2 \\mid \\cdots$. Row operations change the basis of the target $\\mathbb{Z}^4$ and column operations change the basis of the source $\\mathbb{Z}^3$, and both are needed. Pad $M$ with a fourth zero column to make it $4 \\times 4$. This adds nothing to the image, and the missed coordinate then appears as a zero pivot.\n\nReducing $M$ (subtract multiples of column $1$ from the other columns to clear the first row, then continue in the same way) gives\n$$\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix},$$\nwith invariant factors $2, 2, 4$, where $2 \\mid 2 \\mid 4$, and the zero pivot gives the free part.\n\nEach $d_i$ gives a cyclic summand $\\mathbb{Z}/d_i$ and the zero pivot gives a free summand $\\mathbb{Z}$, so\n$$\\mathbb{Z}^4 / H \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/2 \\oplus \\mathbb{Z}/2 \\oplus \\mathbb{Z}/4 \\oplus \\mathbb{Z}.$$"
 },
 "linalg": {
  "1": "We count the conjugacy classes of $3 \\times 3$ matrices $A$ over $\\mathbb{F}_7$ with $A^9 = I$.\n\n$A^9 = I$ means the minimal polynomial of $A$ divides $x^9 - 1$. Since $\\gcd(7, 9) = 1$ this polynomial is squarefree, so $A$ is diagonalizable over the splitting field and its class is determined by the multiset of its elementary divisors. Each elementary divisor is an irreducible factor of $x^9 - 1$ over $\\mathbb{F}_7$, and their degrees sum to $3$.\n\nSo it remains to factor $x^9 - 1$. The irreducible factors correspond to the orbits of the Frobenius map $\\zeta \\mapsto \\zeta^7$ on the $9$th roots of unity, and the degree of a factor is the length of its orbit. Group the roots by order.\n\n<b>Order $1$:</b> The root $1$ is fixed by Frobenius, so its orbit has length $1$. This gives one linear factor, $x - 1$.\n<b>Order $3$:</b> There are two primitive cube roots of unity. Since $7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$, Frobenius fixes each, so the orbits have length $1$. This gives two linear factors, $(x - 2)(x - 4)$.\n<b>Order $9$:</b> There are six primitive $9$th roots of unity. The order of $7$ mod $9$ is $3$ ($7^3 = 343 \\equiv 1$), so each Frobenius orbit has length $3$. This gives $6/3 = 2$ cubic factors.\n\nSo the factors are three distinct linear factors and two distinct cubic factors. A class is a multiset of them of total degree $3$. Break into cases by the partition of $3$.\n\n<b>Case $3 = 1 + 1 + 1$:</b> Three linear factors are chosen with repetition from $\\{x-1, x-2, x-4\\}$. Either all three are equal ($3$ ways), or one is repeated twice and a second is distinct ($3 \\cdot 2 = 6$ ways), or all three are distinct ($1$ way). That is $3 + 6 + 1 = 10$.\n<b>Case $3 = 3$:</b> There is one cubic factor, with $2$ choices.\n\nThe total is $10 + 2 = 12$.",
  "2": "We count the isomorphism classes of $4 \\times 4$ matrices $A$ over $\\mathbb{F}_{11}$ with $A^{20} = I$.\n\n$A^{20} = I$ means $m_A \\mid x^{20} - 1$, where $m_A$ is the minimal polynomial of $A$. Since $\\gcd(11, 20) = 1$ this polynomial is squarefree, so $A$ is diagonalizable over the splitting field and its class is a multiset of irreducible factors of $x^{20} - 1$ over $\\mathbb{F}_{11}$ of total degree $4$.\n\nThe irreducible factors correspond to the orbits of the Frobenius map $\\zeta \\mapsto \\zeta^{11}$ on the $20$th roots of unity, and the degree of a factor is the length of its orbit. Group the roots by order $d \\mid 20$. The orbit length on the roots of order $d$ is the order of $11$ mod $d$.\n\n$d = 1$: The root is $1$. The order of $11$ is $1$, giving one linear factor $x - 1$.\n$d = 2$: The root is $-1$. Here $11 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 2$, giving one linear factor $x + 1$.\n$d = 4$: There are two roots. Here $11 \\equiv 3 \\pmod 4$ has order $2$, giving one quadratic factor.\n$d = 5$: There are four roots. Here $11 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 5$, giving four linear factors.\n$d = 10$: There are four roots. Here $11 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{10}$, giving four linear factors.\n$d = 20$: There are eight roots. Here $11^2 = 121 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{20}$, so $11$ has order $2$, giving $8/2 = 4$ quadratic factors.\n\nThat is $1 + 1 + 4 + 4 = 10$ distinct linear factors and $1 + 4 = 5$ distinct quadratic factors. A class is a multiset of total degree $4$. Break into cases by the partition of $4$ into parts of size $1$ (a linear factor) and $2$ (a quadratic factor).\n\nCase $4 = 1+1+1+1$ (four linear factors): We count multisets of size $4$ from $10$ linear types, sorted by repetition pattern. All four equal gives $10$. One tripled and one single gives $10 \\cdot 9 = 90$. Two doubled gives $\\binom{10}{2} = 45$. One doubled and two single gives $10 \\cdot \\binom{9}{2} = 360$. Four distinct gives $\\binom{10}{4} = 210$. The sum is $10 + 90 + 45 + 360 + 210 = 715$.\nCase $4 = 1+1+2$ (two linear factors and a quadratic factor): The two linear factors form a multiset of size $2$ from $10$ types. There are $10$ equal pairs plus $\\binom{10}{2} = 45$ distinct pairs, so $55$ multisets, and multiplying by the $5$ quadratic factors gives $275$.\nCase $4 = 2+2$ (two quadratic factors): We count multisets of size $2$ from $5$ quadratic types. There are $5$ equal pairs plus $\\binom{5}{2} = 10$ distinct pairs, so $15$.\n\nThe total is $715 + 275 + 15 = 1005$.",
  "3": "We classify the $5 \\times 5$ matrices $A$ over $\\mathbb{F}_2$ with $A^7 = I$, with one representative of each class.\n\nThe minimal polynomial $m_A$ of $A$ satisfies $m_A \\mid x^7 - 1$, which is squarefree since $\\gcd(2, 7) = 1$. So $A$ is diagonalizable over the splitting field and its class is a multiset of irreducible factors of $x^7 - 1$ over $\\mathbb{F}_2$ of total degree $5$.\n\nThe factors correspond to the orbits of the Frobenius map $\\zeta \\mapsto \\zeta^2$ on the $7$th roots of unity.\n\n<b>Order $1$:</b> The root $1$ has orbit length $1$, giving the linear factor $x + 1$.\n<b>Order $7$:</b> There are six primitive $7$th roots of unity. The order of $2$ mod $7$ is $3$ ($2^3 = 8 \\equiv 1$), so each orbit has length $3$, giving $6/3 = 2$ cubic factors. Explicitly\n$$x^7 - 1 = (x + 1)(x^3 + x + 1)(x^3 + x^2 + 1).$$\n\nSo the factors are one linear factor $x + 1$ and two cubic factors $c_1 = x^3 + x + 1$, $c_2 = x^3 + x^2 + 1$. The degree $5$ partitions into parts of size $1$ and $3$ as $5 = 1+1+1+1+1$ or $5 = 1+1+3$, and with only one linear type each pattern is forced. Write $C(c)$ for the companion matrix of $c$.\n\n<b>Case $5 = 1+1+1+1+1$:</b> There are five $(x+1)$ blocks, so $A = I_5$.\nCase $5 = 1+1+3$ with $c_1$: $A = I_2 \\oplus C(c_1)$.\nCase $5 = 1+1+3$ with $c_2$: $A = I_2 \\oplus C(c_2)$.\n\nThere are three classes.",
  "4": "<b>Claim.</b> Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional vector space over a field of characteristic $0$. Then $uv - vu = 1$ is impossible for endomorphisms $u$ and $v$ of $V$.\n\nTake the trace. It is linear and cyclic, that is $\\mathrm{tr}(AB) = \\mathrm{tr}(BA)$, so $\\mathrm{tr}(uv - vu) = 0$. But $\\mathrm{tr}(1) = \\dim V = n$, and $n \\ne 0$ in characteristic $0$. So $uv - vu = 1$ would give $0 = n$, a contradiction.\n\nIn infinite dimension there is no trace to obstruct it. On $K[x]$, with $K$ the field, let $u = \\tfrac{d}{dx}$ and let $v$ be multiplication by $x$. For $x^k$,\n$$uv(x^k) = (x^{k+1})' = (k + 1)x^k, \\qquad vu(x^k) = x \\cdot k x^{k-1} = k x^k,$$\nso $(uv - vu)(x^k) = x^k$. Thus $uv - vu = 1$ holds on the infinite-dimensional space $K[x]$.",
  "5": "$A$ is $10 \\times 10$ with $A^{10000} = 0$, so its minimal polynomial $m_A$ satisfies $m_A \\mid x^{10000}$, which forces $m_A = x^k$ for some $k \\ge 1$.\n\nThe Cayley-Hamilton theorem says $A$ satisfies its characteristic polynomial, $\\chi_A(A) = 0$, and $\\deg \\chi_A = n = 10$. The minimal polynomial divides the characteristic polynomial, that is $x^k \\mid x^{10}$, so $k \\le 10$. The nilpotency index is at most $n = 10$.\n\nTherefore $A^{10} = A^{10 - k} A^k = A^{10 - k} \\cdot 0 = 0$."
 },
 "fields": {
  "1": "$f = x^4 - 3$. Eisenstein's criterion at the prime $3$ applies, since $3$ divides every lower coefficient and $9 \\nmid -3$, so $f$ is irreducible over $\\mathbb{Q}$ and $[\\mathbb{Q}(3^{1/4}) : \\mathbb{Q}] = 4$.\n\nThe roots are $\\pm 3^{1/4}, \\pm i3^{1/4}$, so the splitting field is $K = \\mathbb{Q}(3^{1/4}, i)$. Since $3^{1/4} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ but $i \\notin \\mathbb{R}$, the polynomial $x^2 + 1$ stays irreducible over $\\mathbb{Q}(3^{1/4})$, so $[K : \\mathbb{Q}(3^{1/4})] = 2$. Multiplying the degrees $4$ and $2$ in the tower $\\mathbb{Q} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Q}(3^{1/4}) \\subseteq K$ gives $[K : \\mathbb{Q}] = 8$. As a splitting field of a separable polynomial, $K/\\mathbb{Q}$ is Galois, and $G = \\mathrm{Gal}(K/\\mathbb{Q})$ has $|G| = 8$.\n\nTwo automorphisms generate $G$,\n$$\\sigma : 3^{1/4} \\mapsto i3^{1/4}, \\ i \\mapsto i \\quad (\\text{order } 4), \\qquad \\tau : 3^{1/4} \\mapsto 3^{1/4}, \\ i \\mapsto -i \\quad (\\text{order } 2).$$\nWe check $\\tau \\sigma \\tau : 3^{1/4} \\mapsto \\tau(i3^{1/4}) = -i3^{1/4} = \\sigma^{-1}(3^{1/4})$, so $\\tau \\sigma \\tau = \\sigma^{-1}$.\n\nA group of order $8$ with $\\sigma^4 = \\tau^2 = 1$ and $\\tau \\sigma \\tau = \\sigma^{-1}$ is the dihedral group, so $G \\cong D_8$ (D&F notation, order $8$). This group is nonabelian, since $\\sigma$ and $\\tau$ do not commute.",
  "2": "$f = x^5 - 2$. Eisenstein's criterion at the prime $2$ shows $f$ is irreducible over $\\mathbb{Q}$, so $[\\mathbb{Q}(2^{1/5}) : \\mathbb{Q}] = 5$. Let $\\zeta_5$ be a primitive $5$th root of unity. The cyclotomic field has $[\\mathbb{Q}(\\zeta_5) : \\mathbb{Q}] = \\phi(5) = 4$ with $\\mathrm{Gal}(\\mathbb{Q}(\\zeta_5)/\\mathbb{Q}) \\cong (\\mathbb{Z}/5)^\\times$.\n\nSince $\\gcd(5, 4) = 1$ the two degrees are coprime, so $\\mathbb{Q}(2^{1/5}) \\cap \\mathbb{Q}(\\zeta_5) = \\mathbb{Q}$ and the compositum $K = \\mathbb{Q}(2^{1/5}, \\zeta_5)$ has degree $20$ over $\\mathbb{Q}$. It is the splitting field of $x^5 - 2$ over $\\mathbb{Q}$, hence Galois over $\\mathbb{Q}$, and $G = \\mathrm{Gal}(K/\\mathbb{Q})$ has $|G| = 20$.\n\nThe generators are\n$$\\sigma : 2^{1/5} \\mapsto \\zeta_5 2^{1/5}, \\ \\zeta_5 \\mapsto \\zeta_5 \\quad (\\text{order } 5), \\qquad \\tau : 2^{1/5} \\mapsto 2^{1/5}, \\ \\zeta_5 \\mapsto \\zeta_5^2 \\quad (\\text{order } 4),$$\nusing that $2$ generates $(\\mathbb{Z}/5)^\\times$. Then $\\tau \\sigma \\tau^{-1}(2^{1/5}) = \\zeta_5^2 2^{1/5} = \\sigma^2(2^{1/5})$, so $\\tau \\sigma \\tau^{-1} = \\sigma^2$.\n\nThis is the semidirect product $G \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/5 \\rtimes \\mathbb{Z}/4$, and $\\tau \\sigma \\tau^{-1} = \\sigma^2 \\ne \\sigma$, so $G$ is nonabelian.",
  "3": "$f = x^3 - 3x + 1$. The only candidate rational roots are $\\pm 1$, and $f(1) = -1$, $f(-1) = 3$, so $f$ has no rational root. A cubic with no rational root is irreducible over $\\mathbb{Q}$, so $[\\mathbb{Q}(\\alpha) : \\mathbb{Q}] = 3$ for a root $\\alpha$ of $f$. Let $G$ be the Galois group of the splitting field of $f$ over $\\mathbb{Q}$.\n\nFor a depressed cubic $x^3 + px + q$ the discriminant is $\\Delta = -4p^3 - 27q^2$. Here $p = -3$, $q = 1$, so\n$$\\Delta = -4(-3)^3 - 27(1)^2 = 108 - 27 = 81 = 9^2,$$\na square in $\\mathbb{Q}$.\n\nFor an irreducible cubic, $G \\subseteq S_3$, and $G \\subseteq A_3$ exactly when $\\Delta$ is a square in $\\mathbb{Q}$. Also $3 = [\\mathbb{Q}(\\alpha) : \\mathbb{Q}]$ divides $|G|$. So $G = A_3 \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/3$.",
  "4": "$f = x^3 + 2$. None of $\\pm 1, \\pm 2$ is a root, so the cubic is irreducible over $\\mathbb{Q}$ and $[\\mathbb{Q}(2^{1/3}) : \\mathbb{Q}] = 3$.\n\nLet $\\zeta_3$ be a primitive cube root of unity. The roots are $-2^{1/3}, -\\zeta_3 2^{1/3}, -\\zeta_3^2 2^{1/3}$, so the splitting field is $E = \\mathbb{Q}(2^{1/3}, \\zeta_3)$. Here $[\\mathbb{Q}(\\zeta_3) : \\mathbb{Q}] = 2$ and a real cubic field cannot contain $\\zeta_3$, so the degrees in the tower $\\mathbb{Q} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Q}(2^{1/3}) \\subseteq E$ multiply to give $[E : \\mathbb{Q}] = 3 \\cdot 2 = 6$.\n\nFor the group, the discriminant $\\Delta = -4p^3 - 27q^2$ with $p = 0$, $q = 2$ is $\\Delta = -27 \\cdot 4 = -108$, which is not a square in $\\mathbb{Q}$. An irreducible cubic with non-square discriminant has Galois group $S_3$, so $\\mathrm{Gal}(E/\\mathbb{Q}) \\cong S_3$, acting as all permutations of the three cube roots.",
  "5": "$f = x^4 + 4$. The Sophie Germain identity $a^4 + 4b^4 = (a^2 - 2ab + 2b^2)(a^2 + 2ab + 2b^2)$ with $b = 1$ gives\n$$x^4 + 4 = (x^2 - 2x + 2)(x^2 + 2x + 2),$$\nso $f$ is reducible over $\\mathbb{Q}$. The quadratic formula gives the roots $1 \\pm i$ and $-1 \\pm i$.\n\nAll four roots lie in $\\mathbb{Q}(i)$, so the splitting field is exactly $\\mathbb{Q}(i)$. Then $[\\mathbb{Q}(i) : \\mathbb{Q}] = 2$, so $G = \\mathrm{Gal}(\\mathbb{Q}(i)/\\mathbb{Q})$ has $|G| = 2$, and the nontrivial automorphism is complex conjugation $i \\mapsto -i$. Hence $G \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/2$.",
  "6": "$f = x^7 - 10$. Eisenstein's criterion at the prime $2$ shows $f$ is irreducible over $\\mathbb{Q}$, so $[\\mathbb{Q}(10^{1/7}) : \\mathbb{Q}] = 7$. Let $\\zeta_7$ be a primitive $7$th root of unity. Then $[\\mathbb{Q}(\\zeta_7) : \\mathbb{Q}] = \\phi(7) = 6$ with $\\mathrm{Gal}(\\mathbb{Q}(\\zeta_7)/\\mathbb{Q}) \\cong (\\mathbb{Z}/7)^\\times$.\n\nSince $\\gcd(7, 6) = 1$ the compositum $K = \\mathbb{Q}(10^{1/7}, \\zeta_7)$ has degree $42$ over $\\mathbb{Q}$, and it is the splitting field of $x^7 - 10$ over $\\mathbb{Q}$, hence Galois over $\\mathbb{Q}$. Let $G = \\mathrm{Gal}(K/\\mathbb{Q})$.\n\nThe generators are\n$$\\sigma : 10^{1/7} \\mapsto \\zeta_7 10^{1/7}, \\ \\zeta_7 \\mapsto \\zeta_7 \\quad (\\text{order } 7), \\qquad \\tau : 10^{1/7} \\mapsto 10^{1/7}, \\ \\zeta_7 \\mapsto \\zeta_7^3 \\quad (\\text{order } 6),$$\nwhere $3$ generates $(\\mathbb{Z}/7)^\\times$. Conjugation by $\\tau$ multiplies the exponent by $3$, so $\\tau \\sigma \\tau^{-1} = \\sigma^3$.\n\nSo $G \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/7 \\rtimes \\mathbb{Z}/6$, which is nonabelian.",
  "7": "$f = x^5 - 7$. Eisenstein's criterion at the prime $7$ shows $f$ is irreducible over $\\mathbb{Q}$, so $[\\mathbb{Q}(7^{1/5}) : \\mathbb{Q}] = 5$, while $[\\mathbb{Q}(\\zeta_5) : \\mathbb{Q}] = \\phi(5) = 4$ with group $(\\mathbb{Z}/5)^\\times$, where $\\zeta_5$ is a primitive $5$th root of unity. Since the degrees are coprime, $K = \\mathbb{Q}(7^{1/5}, \\zeta_5)$ has degree $20$ over $\\mathbb{Q}$, and it is Galois over $\\mathbb{Q}$. Let $G = \\mathrm{Gal}(K/\\mathbb{Q})$.\n\nAs before, $\\sigma : 7^{1/5} \\mapsto \\zeta_5 7^{1/5}$, $\\zeta_5 \\mapsto \\zeta_5$ has order $5$, $\\tau : 7^{1/5} \\mapsto 7^{1/5}$, $\\zeta_5 \\mapsto \\zeta_5^2$ has order $4$, and $\\tau \\sigma \\tau^{-1} = \\sigma^2$. So $G \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/5 \\rtimes \\mathbb{Z}/4$.\n\nThe group $G$ is not abelian, since $\\tau \\sigma \\tau^{-1} = \\sigma^2 \\ne \\sigma$.\n\nFor solvability, recall that a polynomial is solvable by radicals iff its Galois group is solvable, and that a group is solvable when its derived series reaches $1$. We compute $[G, G]$. The commutator $[\\tau, \\sigma] = \\tau \\sigma \\tau^{-1} \\sigma^{-1} = \\sigma^2 \\sigma^{-1} = \\sigma$ lies in $[G, G]$, so $\\langle \\sigma \\rangle \\subseteq [G, G]$. And $G/\\langle \\sigma \\rangle \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/4$ is abelian, so $[G, G] \\subseteq \\langle \\sigma \\rangle$. Hence $[G, G] = \\langle \\sigma \\rangle \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/5$, which is abelian, so the derived series $G \\supset \\langle \\sigma \\rangle \\supset 1$ reaches $1$. So $G$ is solvable.",
  "8": "$G = \\mathrm{Gal}(K/E)$ with $|G| = 72 = 2^3 \\cdot 3^2$. The fundamental theorem of Galois theory matches the intermediate fields $E \\subsetneq F \\subsetneq K$ that are Galois over $E$ with the proper nontrivial normal subgroups $N \\trianglelefteq G$, via $F = K^N$ with $[F : E] = |G/N|$. So it suffices to produce such an $N$.\n\nSylow's theorem says the number $n_3$ of Sylow $3$-subgroups of $G$ satisfies $n_3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$ and $n_3 \\mid 8$, so $n_3 \\in \\{1, 4\\}$.\n\n<b>Case 1:</b> If $n_3 = 1$, the Sylow $3$-subgroup (of order $9$) is normal in $G$. Take $N$ to be this subgroup. Then $F = K^N$ has $[F : E] = 72/9 = 8$ and is Galois over $E$.\n\n<b>Case 2:</b> If $n_3 = 4$, let $G$ act by conjugation on its four Sylow $3$-subgroups, giving $\\rho : G \\to S_4$. The action is transitive, so $\\rho$ is nontrivial and $N = \\ker \\rho \\ne G$, while $|N| \\ge 72/24 = 3$. So $N$ is a proper nontrivial normal subgroup of $G$, and $K^N/E$ is the intermediate Galois extension.\n\nIn either case such an $F$ exists.",
  "9": "$G = \\mathrm{Gal}(K/F)$ with $|G| = 36 = 2^2 \\cdot 3^2$. The Galois correspondence matches the subgroups $H \\le G$ with the intermediate fields $K^H$, where $[K : K^H] = |H|$ and $K^H/F$ is Galois iff $H \\trianglelefteq G$.\n\n<b>Part (a):</b> Sylow's theorem gives a subgroup $P_2$ of order $4$ and a subgroup $P_3$ of order $9$. Set $E_1 = K^{P_2}$ and $E_2 = K^{P_3}$. Then $[K : E_1] = |P_2| = 4$ and $[K : E_2] = |P_3| = 9$.\n\n<b>Part (b):</b> For an intermediate field Galois over $F$ we want a proper nontrivial normal subgroup of $G$. Sylow's theorem says the number $n_3$ of Sylow $3$-subgroups of $G$ satisfies $n_3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$ and $n_3 \\mid 4$, so $n_3 \\in \\{1, 4\\}$.\n\n<b>Case 1:</b> If $n_3 = 1$, then $P_3$ is normal in $G$ and $K^{P_3}/F$ is Galois of degree $|G/P_3| = 4$.\n\n<b>Case 2:</b> If $n_3 = 4$, the conjugation action on the four Sylow $3$-subgroups gives $G \\to S_4$ with kernel $N$, where $N$ is nontrivial ($|N| \\ge 36/24 > 1$) and proper. So $K^N/F$ is the desired Galois subextension.",
  "10": "Let $\\overline{\\mathbb{Q}}$ be an algebraic closure of $\\mathbb{Q}$ and $K = \\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt p : p \\text{ prime}) \\subseteq \\overline{\\mathbb{Q}}$.\n\nIndependence of the radicals: For distinct primes $p_1, \\dots, p_n$, $[\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{p_1}, \\dots, \\sqrt{p_n}) : \\mathbb{Q}] = 2^n$. Induct, using that $\\sqrt{p_{k+1}} \\notin \\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{p_1}, \\dots, \\sqrt{p_k})$, since in a multiquadratic field the only square roots of squarefree integers are products of the $\\sqrt{p_i}$, and $p_{k+1}$ is a new prime.\n\nSo each sign choice $\\varepsilon : \\{\\text{primes}\\} \\to \\{\\pm 1\\}$ defines an automorphism $\\sigma_\\varepsilon$ of $K$ with $\\sigma_\\varepsilon(\\sqrt p) = \\varepsilon(p)\\sqrt p$. (Check it on each finite subextension, where it is one of the $2^n$ automorphisms.) Distinct $\\varepsilon$ give distinct $\\sigma_\\varepsilon$, and there are $2^{\\aleph_0}$ sign sequences, so $K$ has uncountably many automorphisms over $\\mathbb{Q}$.\n\nThe isomorphism extension theorem says every automorphism of the algebraic extension $K/\\mathbb{Q}$ extends to an automorphism of $\\overline{\\mathbb{Q}}$, and distinct automorphisms of $K$ extend to distinct ones. So $\\mathrm{Aut}(\\overline{\\mathbb{Q}}/\\mathbb{Q})$ is uncountable."
 }
}
